   "text": "Read only the page below. Read no other file, and do not run commands or browse. Answer each question from the page alone. When the page does not say, answer \"Not stated\".\n\n<<<PAGE\n# See which process held a port\n\nportlog records which process held each local TCP port over time. Use it to answer “what was on port 3000 an hour ago?” after the process has exited.\n\n```sh\nbrew install example/tap/portlog\n```\n\nmacOS and Linux · Written in Go · MIT license\n\n## Try it\n\nStart recording, then look up port 3000:\n\n```sh\nportlog start\nportlog who 3000\n```\n\n## Commands\n\n### Record port history\n\n`portlog start` runs a background recorder. Stop it with:\n\n```sh\nportlog stop\n```\n\n### Look up a port\n\n`portlog who 3000` shows every process that held port 3000, newest first.\n\nLimit the results to the last two hours:\n\n```sh\nportlog who 3000 --since 2h\n```\n\n### Free a port\n\nStop the process that holds port 3000 now, after confirmation:\n\n```sh\nportlog free 3000\n```\n\nAdd `-y` to skip confirmation:\n\n```sh\nportlog free 3000 -y\n```\n\n## Good to know\n\n### History retention\n\nportlog keeps history for 7 days in `~/.portlog/`.\n\n### Command-line arguments\n\nportlog never records command-line arguments, because they can hold secrets.\nPAGE>>>\n\nQuestions:\n(a) What does portlog do?\n(b) What do you run first after installing it?\n(c) Can it show a process that already exited?\n\nReply with only JSON: {\"a\": \"...\", \"b\": \"...\", \"c\": \"...\"}. Start the answer to (c) with Yes, No, or Not stated.",
